EXT. MONASTERY ORCHARD - TRENCH LINE - EVENING

A line of earth cut through rows of apple trees, their blossoms falling like a slow snow into the mud. Beyond the wall, the white stones of a ruined abbey. The sky is the colour of old pewter. Everything is hushed, as though the war has paused to breathe.

LT. HENRI DUBOIS, 30s, sits on an upturned crate at the lip of the trench, a bandage around his left hand, scribbling in a notebook. Thin as a rake, with a poet's brow and a smoker's cough.

SGT. ALBERT QUIGLEY, 50s, a barrel-chested veteran with a drooping moustache, squats beside a battered kettle over a spirit stove. He fusses with the tea, humming under his breath.

PVT. SANTIAGO MORALES, 20, round-faced, sits on the firing step with a harmonica he does not play. He turns it over and over in his hands.

CPL. JOSEPH WHITEFEATHER, 30s, tall, quiet, leans against the trench wall cleaning a rifle with a rag, eyes half-closed, a long braid down his back.

QUIGLEY
Tea's nearly ready.

DUBOIS
Lovely.

QUIGLEY
It's terrible tea.

DUBOIS
Then it's British.

QUIGLEY
(chuckling)
I'm from Leeds, sir. It's worse than British.

Morales lifts the harmonica to his lips, hesitates, lowers it. Whitefeather glances over without comment, then returns to the rifle. A petal drifts into the kettle. Quigley fishes it out with a finger.

MORALES
Sergeant, is it true what they say about the orchard?

QUIGLEY
What do they say?

MORALES
That the monks planted these trees four hundred years ago.

QUIGLEY
Five hundred.

DUBOIS
Closer to six, I believe. The brothers kept records.

MORALES
Records of what?

DUBOIS
Of every harvest. Every frost. Every war that passed through.

WHITEFEATHER
(quietly)
Did they write about us?

DUBOIS
Not yet.

A rumble far off, then silence again. The four men listen. A single blackbird begins to sing from the abbey wall, and Morales finally plays three soft notes on the harmonica before dropping his hand, embarrassed, while Quigley pours the tea into dented tin mugs, hands one to Dubois, one to Whitefeather, one to Morales, and settles back on his heels with the last mug warming his palms, all of them listening to the bird as the light goes slowly copper over the blossoming wall.

QUIGLEY
Funny thing.

DUBOIS
Mm?

QUIGLEY
I've never heard a bird sing in a war before.

WHITEFEATHER
They only sing when it's over.

QUIGLEY
It's not over.

WHITEFEATHER
No.

The blackbird sings on. Dubois closes his notebook and lifts his mug in a small salute to the trees.
